            <div xml:id="d861">

                <pb facs="0780" n="773" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg773"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.72/65: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Hankow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-12-07T17:00:00+08:00">December 7, 1937—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 7—1:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>21. Shanghai’s 1059, December 1, midnight.<anchor n="86" xml:id="d861-f1"/> In a
                    communication of December 4 the Ministry of Foreign Affairs protested against
                    Japanese action in seizing telegraph stations and Bureau of International
                    Telegraphs at Shanghai and in appointing censors to function therein; the
                    Ministry protested also the action of the Municipal Council in failing to
                    prevent such Japanese action and in actually cooperating with the Japanese. I
                    shall reply to this communication merely by saying that its contents have been
                    brought to the attention of the American Consul General at Shanghai.</p>
                <p>Sent to Shanghai and Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
